110
00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:49.780
Amit: So, thanks, Antonio, for giving me the opportunity, and today I would like to share our work on Falcon, on Cortex-M4, which is an efficient and secure lattice-based signature, and this is joint work with Steph and Tim from Lido University, and this was submitted to CHESS 2027.

111
00:14:50.640 --> 00:15:02.599
Amit: So, first, let's look at the GPV framework. It gives us a way to construct secure lattice signatures using full domain hash, which is also known as hash and sign paradigm.

112
00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:12.180
Amit: The signature is just a short pre-image of the hashed message, so we have a function of the signature which is equal to this hashed message.

113
00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:15.449
Amit: And this function is… is…

114
00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:24.649
Amit: called the preimageSampleable, trap.function, which just means that with this function, we can find pre-images,

115
00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:44.319
Amit: a lot of pre-images, and the relation of this function with the signature is given by a Q varices lattice, where the function is just AS mod Q, and here, the A matrix is a fat matrix, which is… which also means that we have a lot of inputs which map to only few outputs.

116
00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:47.679
Amit: So now, all those,

117
00:15:47.740 --> 00:16:06.239
Amit: All those inputs are some short vectors, which form a coset, T plus this orthogonal QRS lattice, and this T is just an easy integer solution for our original AT module Q equation, which is equal to the hashed message.

118
00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:18.639
Amit: So now, let's see how we can build secure and efficient cryptography from GPV. So, first, the public key is just a hard basis, which is close to this uniform matrix A.

119
00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:19.820
Amit: And…

120
00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:27.059
Amit: A good basis is this, capital B matrix, which is orthogonal to A and has a small norm.

121
00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:37.640
Amit: And this good basis is exactly the secret key, or trapdoor, which lets us sample a lot of pre-images, from the solution of corsets.

122
00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:53.670
Amit: And finding the signature just amounts finding some close vector, such that the signature B minus the target T is short, and by shortness, we mean that the N2 norm is bounded by some parameter beta.

123
00:16:55.630 --> 00:17:04.999
Amit: So, to find these short vectors, usually this is an expensive process, but the GPV framework, uses, Gaussian sampler.

124
00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:13.300
Amit: which, use this randomized variant of Babais, plain nearest plane algorithm, which runs in, n-square complexity.

125
00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:15.150
Amit: So…

126
00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:26.020
Amit: Naively, we could just round off each coefficient of the vector and find an integer solution, but that would not give us the closest vector.

127
00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:36.830
Amit: Now, Bhawaii's nearest plane algorithm gives us a closens vector, but we need some randomness to hide the geometry of our secret view.

128
00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:49.719
Amit: And this noise should be appropriate so that, first, it doesn't leak the secret key, and also that it should not be too large, so that the sampler is still secure.

129
00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:56.269
Amit: So now, Falcon uses this CPGPB framework,

130
00:17:56.550 --> 00:18:01.129
Amit: To minimize both the byte count of public key and the signatures.

131
00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:10.199
Amit: And the first, the key, key generation, requires solving this NTRE equation, which is, this fg minus F relation.

132
00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:19.949
Amit: So here, the small polynomials, which are denoted by the small F and Gs, can be sampled independently from these discrete Gaussians.

133
00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:35.440
Amit: But finding the capital, polynomials, F and G, is usually a bit, expensive. So Falcon uses this cyclotomic polynomial, which helps us efficiently calculate, this, this, these polynomials.

134
00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:55.020
Amit: And intuitively, it leverages this, structure of, sag polynomial, and it goes to smaller and smaller rings, and once it finds, capital F and G polynomials for… adds for some integer relation, then it bootstraps itself to, a polynomial ring.

135
00:18:56.560 --> 00:19:11.149
Amit: So now this single Falcon signature is, just a randomized version of GPV, where the capital A matrix is given by this public key, and the important point is that public key is now just a single polynomial.

136
00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:25.749
Amit: And the trapper function now maps this, elements, S1 and S2, to the hash of both the message and the randomness, and this is what we mean by the randomized version of ZKP.

137
00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:35.470
Amit: So now, valid short signature is just, these S1 and S2 ring elements, which are bounded by some, bound beta.

138
00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:46.729
Amit: So Falcon, uses FFTs to speed up operations, and using the structure of these anti-view lattices, it… it can sample,

139
00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:53.629
Amit: It can sample the preimages in n log n time, which is better than the quadratic complexity which we had before.

140
00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:55.419
Amit: From GPV.

141
00:19:55.750 --> 00:20:10.470
Amit: And intuitively, what it does, it takes the target point, and it breaks… splits it, recursively until it reaches the falcon leaves, and then from there it, goes, to the left-hand side.

142
00:20:11.980 --> 00:20:24.520
Amit: So this is the full sampling algorithm for Falcon, and here we can see that it uses these splitting and merge operators to recursively split the target points.

143
00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:28.950
Amit: And if you look at the equation, T0, plus

144
00:20:29.010 --> 00:20:47.149
Amit: this L times T1 minus N1. So here it takes the information from the file country, which is just a LDL decomposition of the z-thread key, and it updates the center so that now we have some randomized, randomized center… randomized center or target.

145
00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:52.620
Amit: And Falcon, so…

146
00:20:52.820 --> 00:21:10.030
Amit: to keep the RAM footprint low, we can… we can compute the Falcon tree on the fly, so usually we have, like, two implementations of Falcon. One is a precomputer tree, and one is where the tree is computed on the fly, which helps, keep the RAM low.

147
00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:19.359
Amit: And for security, the leaf value should be between this, sigma, some standard deviation for security.

148
00:21:20.910 --> 00:21:34.530
Amit: So, the signature security for Falcon reduces to some, unfortunately under chosen message attack, to multi-targeting homogeneity assumption, while the key security depends on the NTRU assumption.

149
00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:41.399
Amit: And it is interesting that NTRE assumption there is no secretive proof for it, so it is, still an open problem.

150
00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:57.019
Amit: And the Falcon introduces additional constraints on the key generation, so now the keys are not the perfect Gaussian, or not the perfect banker that we are used… that we are used to from Gaussians.

151
00:21:57.020 --> 00:22:03.979
Amit: Now, they've skewed a little bit, but till now, there hasn't been any attacks on Falcon, leveraging this,

152
00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:05.280
Amit: shortcoming.

153
00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:15.519
Amit: And also, it is very interesting to note that the current parameters of Falcon are not sufficient in the QROM model, and this all has been, like, recent work in the past year.

154
00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:30.150
Amit: So, the limitation of the Falcon Standard was this, dependency on floating-point arithmetic. And more importantly, many embedded processors lack a floating-point unit.

155
00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:39.640
Amit: Masking a floating-point arithmetic is an open problem, so Falcon, in its original form, is vulnerable to sites and attacks.

156
00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:47.070
Amit: And you… and probably this is the reason why the standard hasn't been approved for the past 3 years.

157
00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:55.809
Amit: So now, let's look at the overhead from floating point operations. So first, the floating point division is not constant time.

158
00:22:56.080 --> 00:23:01.299
Amit: We cannot deploy it in security-critical operations, like online signatures.

159
00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:04.959
Amit: Floating-point divisions are very expensive to mask, so…

160
00:23:05.380 --> 00:23:11.820
Amit: They incur, like, 100x overhead, when we try to mask these floating-point divisions.

161
00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:20.659
Amit: And… And… One of the ways that we could emulate all the floating-point operations with integers

162
00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:30.090
Amit: But this still incurs a 20x overhead, so performance takes a lot of hit when we want, it to run on constrained devices.

163
00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:35.490
Amit: So, a natural question to ask was that, can we retain the efficiency of

164
00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:39.139
Amit: Belkin, while avoiding these floating-point operations.

165
00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:42.610
Amit: And the recent work this past year,

166
00:23:43.190 --> 00:24:01.489
Amit: made a fixed-point implementation of Falcon, where they recasted the sample security using the absolute bounds. And what this allowed was that now the key generation, the errors that happen in the key generation, we can control them.

167
00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:09.260
Amit: Using… using that, it allows us to bound every intermediated banners in the computation, so that they don't overflow.

168
00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:20.260
Amit: And this allows us… this allows a fixed point, implementation of Falcon, because now we have a bound on every intermediate value, so we can fix a scale.

169
00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:24.860
Amit: And we can essentially perform all the computation in fixed-point arithmetic.

170
00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:34.090
Amit: And the paper also showed that if the Falcon has some lambda-bit security, then the fixed-point version only loses 2 bit of security.

171
00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:46.959
Amit: So, one practical format that, fixed point, is very efficient is… is this 3264 format, because it is amenable to, modern processors.

172
00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:50.080
Amit: And again, fixed point,

173
00:24:50.880 --> 00:25:05.949
Amit: Fixed-point arithmetic, we can… we can see it as having some fixed fraction part and fixed integer part, whereas in floating-point operations, we usually have the fraction part, varies according to the operations.

174
00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:21.719
Amit: So now, what were the bottlenecks, for, the fixed point operations? So, after every multiplication, we need to round to the nearest interior, because when we multiply, two fixed-point numbers, we…

175
00:25:21.790 --> 00:25:28.190
Amit: The scale changes, so that… so we need to rescale, and this needed, additional rounding.

176
00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:40.059
Amit: Moreover, the inverse and square root operations are now implemented using Newton-Raphson, so if you use Newton-Raphson instead of exact division or square root.

177
00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:53.500
Amit: They involve a lot of operation, a lot of multiplications. And since we have this overhead for rounding, we… we would have, say, suppose, 6 or 7, times round… times rounding, in…

178
00:25:53.590 --> 00:25:55.840
Amit: One inverse or square root combination.

179
00:25:56.660 --> 00:26:11.889
Amit: So what we found out, that we could instead delay the rounding, and so… so what we can do is that we can keep wide limbs, or we can keep some buffer limbs, and add some extra precision so that, we can delay the rounding till the end.

180
00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:21.970
Amit: And this also lets us fuse some operations, like inverse and multiplications, and round only once, at the end. So… so we do…

181
00:26:22.310 --> 00:26:32.920
Amit: operations in versus multiplications, with some wide limb format, and only at the end, we round them, and this improves both speed and precision.

182
00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:47.680
Amit: And usually rounding takes around 10 to 15 cycles on Cortex-M4, which is very, which is a bit… which becomes a bit, expensive when we do a lot of multiplications.

183
00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:53.779
Amit: Because usually multiply and accumulate is only a single cycle annual.

184
00:26:54.720 --> 00:27:03.750
Amit: So here were the numbers, compared from the floating point and the fixed point. So we can see that the additional surprises are very cheap now.

185
00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:06.889
Amit: But, we can see that,

186
00:27:07.250 --> 00:27:18.529
Amit: The complex multiplication and reciprocals are very expensive in the floating point operations, because to do exact divisions in floating point, gets very expensive.

187
00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:21.670
Amit: So we, so in Fixed Point,

188
00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:34.840
Amit: we, did this, Newton-Dobson, reciprocal and square root, and we also delayed the rounding till the very end. So we were able to get a lot of speed up on almost, like.

189
00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:50.389
Amit: almost, like, double speedup in complex multiplication, 4 times for reciprocal, and this all, this all improves our F… this all improves the operations, FFT operations, which are very expensive in Franklin.

190
00:27:51.580 --> 00:28:04.990
Amit: So, for performance, using the 3264 format, we were able to get, close to 9.1 million cycles for Falcon 5112, and 19.3 million cycles for 1024.

191
00:28:05.110 --> 00:28:13.359
Amit: And now, the important point is that it is only 2x lower than Dilithium, which was previously, like, 4x for a single sign-in trial.

192
00:28:13.470 --> 00:28:20.950
Amit: And more importantly, it also allowed us to improve the precision, which now allows, like, to report 99 queries.

193
00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:27.570
Amit: From the previous number of 2.56, which was lacking behind the list, threshold.

